    "explanation": "Not today. Red flag - the water is closed. This is the kind of thing that genuinely changes during the day, so it's worth a re-check before you write the afternoon off entirely.\n\nThere's a modest storm signal, peaking near 29% around 6 PM. That is the normal South Florida summer pattern rather than a washout - it usually means a cell builds, rains hard somewhere nearby for twenty minutes, and moves on. The rule that matters is lightning: flags say nothing about it, so get out of the water at the first rumble and wait half an hour after the last one.\n\nFor what it's worth, the county rip current risk is rated high. That number is a forecast written before dawn for the whole coastline and it regularly disagrees with the flag actually flying at Pompano, so treat it as background, not a veto."
